Go.dot patch 0001 for JUCE: a String built from malformed UTF-8 stays inside its allocation.

JUCE's own fix, commit 330ef16fa0d04bf36d843c7811e0f1d5a31b2c66 on develop
("String: Fix allocation sizes for malformed data", Tom Poole, 2026-10-08),
released in 9.0.4 and, cherry-picked as 619353fb327b, in 8.0.16 - the only
change 8.0.16 makes. Taken verbatim from 619353fb327b: juce_String.cpp is the
same at this pin as at that commit's parent, so every hunk is JUCE's.

The fault: StringHolderUtils sized a new string's allocation by one walk of its
source and filled it by another, and on malformed UTF-8 the two disagree, so the
copy writes past the end of the heap block. A String made from a std::string
counted one byte for each source byte, then wrote each malformed byte (a lone
continuation byte, say) as U+FFFD, three bytes. A String made from a bare UTF-8
pointer counted three bytes for a malformed four-byte sequence (an overlong one)
and copied its four. Go.dot builds such strings from bytes it does not control:
every WebSocket message juce_simpleweb receives on the OSCQuery port
(String(in_message->string())), show files (CanonicalXml.cpp), and text cue
words, which an OSC message can set (String::fromUTF8 in TextRaster.cpp). The
fix sizes and copies in one walk.

JUCE's test for the fix comes with it, in the same file. Go.dot's own,
tests/ToolchainTests.cpp, reads the allocation a malformed string was given, so
a build without this patch fails there. Each hunk carries a notice that Go.dot
modified the file (GPL-3 and AGPL-3, section 5(a)).

LINE ENDINGS. JUCE stores juce_String.cpp with CRLF in its own repository, so
every line of the hunks below ends in CRLF and must stay so; .gitattributes
keeps this file byte for byte (-text), and scripts/patches.py writes it as bytes.

Applied by Go.dot's build, cmake/WfgJucePatches.cmake, in the order
patches/JUCE/series gives; refreshed from the working tree with
scripts/patches.py JUCE refresh, which keeps this header. Against JUCE
37c894f83d37 (8.0.13+7, the JUCE Tracktion Engine 13b5132 pins). Drop it when
the pin moves to 8.0.16, 9.0.4 or later. The author's decision of 2026-10-14.

